course: dictionary
lesson: 9598
title: Strew: scattered and messy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To strew something is to scatter it untidily over a surface, so it lies in many places rather than in one neat pile. Look at the clues around the word. «The Woman picked out a nice dry Cave, instead of a heap of wet leaves, strewed clean sand» tells you that sand was spread across the floor, not piled up in a heap.
explain: Strew can also describe a place that is covered with things lying about. Read «toys strewn across the floor» and you can picture the floor covered with toys, all over the place. Strewn is a past form of strew, so it describes what has already been scattered.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. If "scattered" fits and still makes sense, you have probably found the right meaning.
fact: Strew means to scatter things untidily over a surface.
fact: Strew can also mean to be lying scattered over a place, as in «leaves strewed the path».
fact: Strew has two past forms: strewed and strewn. Both are correct.
fact: «Strewn with» is a common phrase, as in «the beach was strewn with rubbish», meaning covered with things scattered about.
fact: Strew is a describing verb for messy or scattered scenes, such as toys on the floor or petals at a wedding.
exercise: In «strewed clean sand», what does strewed mean? | *Scattered over the floor | Washed out by the sea | Swept into one heap | Dug up and carried away
exercise: «Toys were strewn across the floor.» What does strewn tell you about the toys? | *Scattered about in a messy way | Hidden under the bed | Packed neatly in a box
exercise: Yesterday the wind ___ leaves over the path. Which word fits best? | stroked | stretched | strode | *strewed
test: Petals were ___ along the aisle at the wedding. What does strewn mean here? | *Scattered along the aisle | Pressed flat in a book | Blown away by the wind | Gathered into a basket
test: Which sentence means the beach was covered with rubbish lying about? | *The beach was strewn with rubbish | The beach was quiet and empty | The beach was painted blue | The beach was swept clean
exam: Which word is the closest in meaning to strew? | fold | gather | *scatter | stack
